{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Plan-review: PASS (certified) — and disposition of its 5 advisories\n\n`review-plan` history, all run unbounded, none under `timeout`:\n\n| run | verdict | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| v1 | BLOCK | E6/COH — AC14 said \"a make lint gate\" while DD1 verified a script no criterion mandated creating |\n| v2 | BLOCK | **P4 deterministic floor** — description 8840 chars > 8000 admission cap |\n| v3 | **PASS** | 0 blocking, 5 advisory |\n\nv3's attestation did not persist on its own (`attestation: null` on a signable PASS — the\ndocumented failure mode). Recovered with `sign-review` (no LLM re-run):\n`{\"ok\": true, \"signed\": true, \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"head_sha\": \"ce3b62144aeb...\"}`, and\n`review-plan --status` now reports **`certified`**.\n\nConfirmed empirically that ticking checkboxes is attestation-safe: `--status` still reports\n`certified` after ticking AC1-AC16 and DD1-DD6. Prose edits are what invalidate it.\n\n### Advisory dispositions\n\n**1. E6 — DD1's proving command is weaker than it looks. ACCEPTED, and worth stating plainly.**\nCorrect, and the most useful finding of the five. `check_tickets_boundary.py` is STATIC: it\nproves the *absence* of hardcoded composition, not that the mutating paths AC1-AC7 read and\nwrite the right place at runtime. My behavioural proof (held-out test + mutation check) covers\n**AC8/AC9 only** — the `bridge_status` path with the confirmed live failure. AC1-AC7 and\nAC10-AC13 rest on the static gate, 4276 passing tests, and diff inspection. That is reasonable\nevidence but it is **not** relocation-specific behavioural proof, and I am not claiming it is.\nA relocation-parameterised reconciler test would close this; I did not write one.\n\n**2. G6 — commit-back with an out-of-repo store. REFUTED for this change.**\n`reconcile_helpers.py` commits via `commit_tickets_branch(tracker_dir, …)`\n(`_store/push.py`), which takes the store as its target and so operates wherever it lives.\nThe advisory's concern is about the *GHA workflow* commit-back step staging the repo worktree —\na pre-existing deployment constraint, not something this change introduces. In CI the store is\nat the repo root by default, so the workflow path is unaffected.\n\n**3. G1G2 — a second caller. ALREADY COVERED, and the check was worth running.**\n`_cli/_bridge_commands.py:189` does independently pass `Path(config.repo_root())` into\n`last_pass.snapshot`. It needs no change because **the fix is at the callee**: the `_store_dir()`\nseam inside `last_pass.py` resolves the store regardless of which surface calls in. That is the\nshared-core shape the parity sweep prefers over per-surface patches, and\n`test_bridge_status_cli_succeeds_against_a_relocated_store` exercises exactly this second\nsurface end-to-end through the real CLI. **Parity census:** CLI ✅ (`_bridge_commands.py`),\nMCP/library ✅ (`_lib_ops.py`), automated callers ✅ (reconciler, same seam) — no MISSING slot.\n\n**4. G6 — AC3 does not name which resolver. ACCEPTED as wording.**\nThe implementation uses `config.tracker_dir()`. The `reconcile.py:452` comment that asserted the\nopposite (\"use the `.tickets-tracker` boundary here — not config.tracker_dir()\") was **removed**\nas part of this change, since it was factually wrong: `ReconcilerSettings.tracker_dir` is exactly\nsuch a resolver.\n\n**5. P6 — AC/DoD quality. ACCEPTED, no action.** Generic; the specific instances are 1 and 4.\n\n### Residual risk\n\n- **Relocation is behaviourally proven only for `bridge_status`** (advisory 1). Recommend a\n  follow-up ticket for a relocation-parameterised reconciler test if the mutating paths matter\n  to a relocated deployment. I did not file it — that is a scoping call for the operator.\n- **A sandboxed pytest run leaks scratch into the worktree.** Denying `/var` writes makes pytest\n  fall back to a `pytest-of-*` basetemp inside the tree. I caught it before staging; anyone\n  repeating the mutation procedure should check `git status` before `git add -A`.\n- **`cca6-3f7e-dd67-439f` remains open**, satisfied-in-substance by this commit but not closed."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787862385989943061,"uuid":"bf52ed87-beb8-4bf5-9bfe-b5913844036a"}